section: lifecycle
title: 'Lifecycle: update, repave, delete'
about: |
  What a change of a running cluster does, and what a delete needs. A rule on a change applies
  with `cluster:UID CHANGE.json`.
rules:
- id: eks-type-change-orphan
  title: A change of the instance type of an EKS node group leaves the old group
  engines:
  - eks
  tier: static
  severity: warn
  status: seen
  when: .before != null
  what: |
    A change of the instance type makes a new node group and keeps the old one, untracked: 4
    nodes where the config asks 2. The delete of the cluster then waits with no end.
  check:
    kind: static
    expr: |
      (.before.spec.machinepoolconfig // []) as $b
      | [ .entity.spec.machinepoolconfig[] | .poolConfig.name as $n | .cloudConfig.instanceType as $t
          | ($b[] | select(.poolConfig.name == $n)) as $old
          | select($old.cloudConfig.instanceType != null and $old.cloudConfig.instanceType != $t)
          | "pool \($n): \($old.cloudConfig.instanceType) to \($t) makes a new node group and leaves the old one" ]
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Sweep the old machine pools.
  evidence:
  - kind: run
    ref: '#89'
  - kind: report
    ref: palette-eks-orphan-nodegroup-delete-stuck-findings-2026-09-28
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/eks/type-change.json
    clean: fixtures/eks/type-same.json
- id: eks-delete-waits
  title: The delete of an EKS cluster reaches Deleted
  engines:
  - eks
  tier: watch
  severity: fail
  status: seen
  what: |
    With an orphan node group, the delete waits with no end. `CloudInfrastructureCleanedUp`
    stays `False` with the reason `DeletingInfrastructure`, and the event "Deleting control
    plane nodes" repeats every 5 minutes. The EKS control plane waits for every node group of
    the cluster, and Palette deletes the one it tracks alone. A delete of the orphan in AWS
    alone does not end the wait: its object keeps its finalizer in the pilot. The recovery is by
    hand: delete the node groups, the EKS cluster, and the VPC in AWS, then force delete.
  check:
    kind: watch
    expr: |
      (.cluster.status.state // "") as $s
      | ([ (.cluster.status.conditions // [])[]
           | select(.type == "CloudInfrastructureCleanedUp" and .status == "False" and (.reason // "") == "DeletingInfrastructure") ] | length > 0) as $waits
      | ([ (.events // [])[] | select((.message // "") | test("Deleting control plane nodes")) ] | length) as $n
      | if $s == "Deleting" and $waits and .elapsed > 1800
        then ["the delete waits at CloudInfrastructureCleanedUp=False, DeletingInfrastructure, for \(.elapsed) s, with \($n) 'Deleting control plane nodes' events: an orphan node group can hold the EKS control plane. Run examples/live/eks-nodegroups.sh; --delete removes the node groups of the example. Then delete the EKS cluster and its VPC in AWS, and force delete the cluster in Palette"]
        else [] end
  reproduce:
  - 'examples/live/eks-nodegroups.sh            # one line for each node group of the cluster; two for one pool is an orphan'
  - 'examples/live/eks-nodegroups.sh --delete   # deletes the node groups of the example, and waits until they are gone'
  - 'aws eks describe-cluster --region {{ .entity.spec.cloudConfig.region }} --name {{ .entity.metadata.name }} --query cluster.status   # ACTIVE while the delete waits'
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Delete each AWSManagedMachinePool that no MachinePool references, in the delete path, before the control plane.
  evidence:
  - kind: run
    ref: '#89'
    note: the state stayed DeletingInfrastructure for more than 2 hours, with the event every 5 minutes
  - kind: report
    ref: palette-eks-orphan-nodegroup-delete-stuck-findings-2026-09-28 F4
  - kind: source
    note: the delete reads the one machine pool that the MachinePool references
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/eks/delete-waits.json
    clean: fixtures/eks/watch-running.json
- id: gke-type-change
  title: A change of the machine type of a GKE node pool deletes the pool
  engines:
  - gke
  tier: static
  severity: fail
  status: seen
  when: .before != null
  what: |
    The new machine pool gets the GKE name of the old one, so the delete of the old one deletes
    the GKE node pool, and nothing makes a new one. The cluster keeps no node, and its delete
    stays `Deleting` until a force delete.
  check:
    kind: static
    expr: |
      (.before.spec.machinepoolconfig // []) as $b
      | [ .entity.spec.machinepoolconfig[] | .poolConfig.name as $n | .cloudConfig.instanceType as $t
          | ($b[] | select(.poolConfig.name == $n)) as $old
          | select($old.cloudConfig.instanceType != null and $old.cloudConfig.instanceType != $t)
          | "pool \($n): \($old.cloudConfig.instanceType) to \($t) deletes the GKE node pool and makes no new one" ]
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Sweep by label, and roll no pool before the control plane is ready.
  evidence:
  - kind: run
    ref: '#90'
  - kind: report
    ref: palette-gke-pool-change-delete-stuck-findings-2026-09-28
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/gke/type-change.json
    clean: fixtures/gke/type-same.json
